<!-- question: 169573 -->
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>MAT322 - Simpson's Error Bound</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Determine the minimum <strong>even</strong> integer \(n\) required to approximate \(\int_{0}^{@b@} e^{@k@x} \, dx\) with an error of less than \({@eps@}\) using the Composite Simpson's Rule.</p>
<p>\(n = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>The error bound for the Composite Simpson's Rule is \(|E| \le \frac{(b-a)^5}{180n^4} \max |f^{(4)}(\xi)|\).</p>
<p>Here \(f(x) = e^{@k@x}\), so \(f^{(4)}(x) = {@k^4@}e^{@k@x}\).</p>
<p>The maximum value of \(|f^{(4)}(x)|\) on \([0, {@b@}]\) is \({@k^4@}e^{@k*b@}\) (since it's increasing).</p>
<p>We need \(\frac{({@b@}-0)^5}{180n^4} ({@k^4@}e^{@k*b@}) < {@eps@}\).</p>
<p>\[ n^4 > \frac{{@b^5*k^4@}e^{@k*b@}}{180({@eps@})} \]</p>
<p>\[ n > \left( \frac{{@b^5*k^4@}e^{@k*b@}}{180({@eps@})} \right)^{1/4} \approx {@val@} \]</p>
<p>Rounding up to the nearest even integer, we get \(n = {@ta1@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber></idnumber>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2025073100</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text>b:1;
k:rand(2)+1;    /* k is 1 or 2 */
eps:0.00001;
max_f4: k^4 * exp(k*b);
val: ( (b^5 * max_f4) / (180 * eps) )^(1/4);
n_min: ceiling(val);
/* Ensure n is even */
ta1: if mod(n_min, 2) = 0 then n_min else n_min + 1;
</text>
